      "dom_id": "question-14160",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14179856/assets/2a195992bbda673f38856ee5b5ea6f0f14f6cf1303df200d0125e29f365a51e3.png)\n\nWhat is the $x$-intercept of the parabola shown above?",
      "worked_solution": "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(1,-2)$ and passes through the point $(5,2).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(1,-2)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y+2)^{2}=4p(x-1).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(5,2),$ we substitute $x=5$ and $y=2$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2+2)^{2} & =4p(5-1) \\\\ 16 & =4p(4) \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+2)^{2} & =4(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola  and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0+2)^{2} & =4(x-1) \\\\ 4 & =4(x-1) \\\\ 1 & =x-1 \\\\ x & =1+1 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $2.$",
