      "worked_solution": "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(4,5)$ and passes through the point $(6,3).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(4,5)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(x-4)^{2}=4p(y-5).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(6,3),$ we substitute $x=6$ and $y=3$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6-4)^{2} & =4p(3-5) \\\\ 4 & =4p(-2) \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =-2(y-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-4)^{2} & =-2(y-5) \\\\ 16 & =-2(y-5) \\\\ -8 & =y-5 \\\\ y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $-3.$",
