      "dom_id": "question-251810",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14179856/assets/31618ee78ebe4834cf76aedebce7c25eca8413c6a4b9d91761ccc38dbd32b01d.png)\n\nThe value of the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above  is{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(2,2)$ and passes through the point $(-2,-2).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(2,2)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}=4p(y-2).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-2,-2),$ we substitute $x=-2$ and $y=-2$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2-2)^{2} & =4p(-2-2) \\\\ 16 & =4p(-4) \\\\ p & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =-4(y-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-2)^{2} & =-4(y-2) \\\\ 4 & =-4(y-2) \\\\ -1 & =y-2 \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $1.$",
